 ### [GGX Microfacet BRDF](3715-ggx-microfacet-brdf.md)
-A physically based specular model using Gaussian microfacet distribution. Choose for high-quality rough surface highlights.
+The GGX Microfacet BRDF is a specular reflection model that approximates surface roughness using the Gaussian distribution of microfacet normals (GGX) combined with the Schlick Fresnel approximation and a Smith geometry function. It is distinguished from its siblings, such as [Blinn-Phong with Metallic Reflections](4252-blinn-phong-with-metallic-reflections.md), by providing physically plausible energy conservation and accurate specular highlights for rough surfaces, whereas Blinn-Phong is an empirical approximation. It serves as the standard for high-quality specular shading in real-time engines and voxel renderers.
 
 ### [Light Evaluation for SDF Faces](3751-light-evaluation-for-sdf-faces.md)
-Analytic lighting applied to discretized SDF faces. Choose for simple, fast face-based shading without full ray integration.
+This method applies lighting (diffuse, specular, and ambient) to a face color based on the surface normal and light direction. It is designed for SDF-based rendering where the "face" represents a discretized surface element. It differs from general volume shading by operating directly on the extracted face geometry's color and alpha, using a simplified analytic lighting model rather than full ray-marched integration.
 
 ### [Voxel Shading Pipeline](3898-voxel-shading-pipeline.md)
-Per-voxel lighting combining diffuse, rim, indirect, and microfacet specular terms with hard shadows. Choose for voxel-based engines requiring discrete specular highlights.
+The Voxel Shading Pipeline is a per-voxel lighting model that computes illumination for surface points identified by ray marching through a voxel grid. It combines Lambertian diffuse reflection, rim lighting, indirect ambient contribution, and a microfacet-based specular term, modulated by hard shadows and tone mapping. It differs from continuous surface shading by operating on discrete voxel coordinates, rounding ray hit positions to the grid to generate per-voxel specular highlights, and employs hard shadows via secondary ray occlusion checks.
 
 ### [Blinn-Phong with Metallic Reflections](4252-blinn-phong-with-metallic-reflections.md)
-Blinn-Phong extended with metallic modulation and environment reflections. Choose for stylized urban night scenes with metallic materials.
+A custom shading model for ray-marched signed distance fields that extends the classic Blinn-Phong approximation with environment reflections and emissive contributions to simulate urban night scenes. It distinguishes itself from standard Blinn-Phong by modulating specular intensity, power, and reflection weight based on a material's metallic property, and from GGX microfacet models by using a simplified Schlick-like Fresnel term rather than a full microfacet distribution.
 
 ### [Fast Shading for Voxel Engines](5606-fast-shading-for-voxel-engines.md)
-CPU-based palette index modification for directional illumination. Choose for voxel engines needing fast, pre-computed shading without ray marching.
+Fast Shading for Voxel Engines (fast_shade2) is a CPU-based lighting algorithm for voxel engines that simulates directional illumination (specifically vertical sun rays) by modifying voxel palette indices. It differs from its sibling [fast_shade1](5608-fast-shade1.md) by using a more complex neighborhood check involving 11 specific neighbors to determine shading intensity, offering higher visual detail at the cost of increased neighbor lookups. It operates directly on the voxel grid data structure rather than rendering a mesh or ray-marching a distance field.
 
 ### [Multidirectional Occlusion Shading](7667-multidirectional-occlusion-shading.md)
-Direct volume rendering using tilted cone phase functions for soft shadows. Choose for interactive DVR with directional light scattering.
+Multidirectional Occlusion Shading (M-DOS) is a direct volume rendering technique that simulates directional light scattering to produce realistic soft-shadowing and depth cues. Unlike the original MS-DOS model, which constrains the light direction to the viewing direction and uses symmetric disc-shaped Gaussian kernels, M-DOS allows arbitrary light positioning within the hemisphere defined by the view vector by using elliptical footprints derived from a tilted cone. It achieves this through incremental convolution of an opacity buffer, requiring no precomputation and maintaining interactive frame rates.
 
 ### [Single-Atomic High-Quality Robust Variant (HQS1R)](10218-single-atomic-high-quality-robust-variant-hqs1r.md)
-Point cloud rendering with atomic blending and overflow protection. Choose for high-quality, anti-aliased point cloud visualization.
+HQS1R is a high-quality point cloud rendering method that reduces aliasing by blending overlapping points together, similar to mipmapping. It is a simplified, compute-based implementation of the blending algorithm from [BHZK05], designed to be faster than aliased `GL_POINTS` results while providing anti-aliasing. Unlike surface splatting [ZPVG01], it blends overlapping points without considering oriented disks with variable radii. It sets itself apart from the [Single-Atomic High-Quality Variant (HQS1x)](#single-atomic-high-quality-variant-hqs1x) by using a robust overflow protection mechanism that prevents artifacts when more than 255 points contribute to a pixel.
 
 ### [YUV Color Space Shading with Glow](11187-yuv-color-space-shading-with-glow.md)
-Illumination in YUV space decoupling luminance and chrominance. Choose for independent control of brightness and color with volumetric glow.
+This method performs volume raycasting illumination in the YUV color space to decouple luminance from chrominance, allowing for independent control of brightness and color. It differs from standard RGB shading by computing lighting effects (such as specular highlights and glow) primarily on the luminance channel, while interpolating color channels based on a technique-derived factor. This approach is particularly useful for capturing indirect illumination independently of the transfer function.
+
+### [Tangent Space Shading](12616-tangent-space-shading.md)
+Tangent Space Shading is a gradient-less lighting technique that approximates the diffuse lighting component of the Phong shading model without computing explicit gradients for every texel. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [GGX Microfacet BRDF](3715-ggx-microfacet-brdf.md) and [Light Evaluation for SDF Faces](3751-light-evaluation-for-sdf-faces.md) by its reliance on rendering artifacts (double-pass texture coordinate offsets) rather than geometric derivatives to simulate surface orientation.
+
+### [Voxel Shader Volumetric Ray Marching](16930-voxel-shader-volumetric-ray-marching.md)
+A shading method for Fire/Smoke simulations and meshes that reads voxel grid data from a Phoenix Simulator or V-Ray Volume Grid. It handles volumetric shading via specific rollouts (Fire, Smoke Color, Smoke Opacity) because standard Maya materials cannot shade volumetrics.
 
 ### Procedural Road Surface
-A procedural surface shader for roads that differentiates between asphalt, curb/shoulder, and terrain based on lateral distance to the road centerline, including emissive lane markings [S2, unofficial].
-1. Calculate $d = p.x - \text{roadX}(p.z)$ and $ad = |d|$ [S2, unofficial].
-2. If $ad < \text{ROAD\_HALF\_WIDTH}$: set albedo to dark gray, metal to $0.35$, emissive to $0.0$. Calculate lane lines using `smoothstep` for edge lines, center line, and dashed separators. Add emissive cyan color for lines [S2, unofficial].
-3. Else if $ad < \text{ROAD\_HALF\_WIDTH} + \text{SHOULDER}$: set albedo to dark gray, metal to $0.1$, emissive to magenta strip at road edge [S2, unofficial].
-4. Else: set albedo to very dark, metal to $0.0$, emissive to $0.0$ [S2, unofficial].
-Parameters: Asphalt metal $0.35$ (wet-look); Lane line color $\text{vec3}(0.15, 1.0, 1.1) \times 1.3$; Edge line offset $\text{ROAD\_HALF\_WIDTH} - 0.3$; Center line offset $0.18$; Dashed line frequency $p.z \times 0.18$ [S2, unofficial].
